# Summary
Introduced: the commit adds a new 250ms production backfill-candidate scan cadence and immediate startup scan without adding per-PG row, byte, or time budgets. The PG limit reduces the number of PGs per tick but does not bound the amount of attacker-controlled metadata loaded from each PG.
The new scheduling splits backfill candidate scans out of the shard-scavenger sweep and runs them immediately, then every 250ms by default. Although the cluster code caps each tick to 8 PGs, each PG scan calls list_shard_scavenger_payload_references(), which returns a Vec built from unbounded SELECTs over object_segments, stream_upload_segments, multipart_part_segments, and reclaim tables. The scanner then inserts those references into a BTreeMap and iterates current-epoch entries before enforcing scan_limit, so ordinary current-epoch objects created by an authenticated client still consume CPU, SQLite IO, and memory every scan cycle. With the default 16 PGs, the worker can rescan the full object metadata population roughly twice per second, compared with the previous 60-second sweep cadence. This creates a remotely triggerable availability degradation path for any principal able to write many objects or multipart parts.

