# Resolution

Validity: valid. The original implementation bounded only the number of PGs
visited per scheduler tick. Within each selected PG it still materialized and
sorted the complete metadata reference set, so authenticated object and
multipart growth could make the 250 ms discovery path consume unbounded
SQLite I/O, CPU, and memory.

Fixed in commit `683c80d9` (`Bound placed-segment backfill discovery`).

The fix replaces full-PG materialization with storage-owned, keyset-paginated
reference discovery:

- object, stream-upload, and multipart-part references are read directly from
  their indexed native tables through opaque resume cursors;
- each storage RPC returns at most 64 references, each worker tick consumes at
  most 256 rows and 256 candidate verifications, and the scanner yields after
  a 50 ms cooperative time budget;
- cursors remain attached to the scanner across runtime-map replacement, while
  route changes and deferred PGs are retried without restarting a full scan;
- pending metadata commands transactionally publish fixed-width child pages of
  at most 64 placed references instead of requiring repeated command decoding
  or SQLite reads of one unbounded BLOB;
- pending-command cursors bind the command epoch, PG, log index, and checksum;
  replacing the slot with a different or shorter command restarts traversal at
  reference zero instead of retaining a permanently invalid offset;
- the existing full shard-scavenger inventory remains on the low-frequency
  audit path rather than the 250 ms candidate-discovery cadence.

Regression coverage includes native keyset traversal, RPC page limits,
candidate and time budgets, cursor retention across runtime-map replacement,
and a 512-reference pending command. The large-command regression verifies
that storage creates eight bounded child BLOBs, does not decode the command on
each page, and restarts correctly after replacement by a shorter command.

